  "subproblems": "1. Model the game as a recursive impartial scoring game on a line segment of length L. Define A(L) as the number of uncovered squares when it is Alice’s turn on a segment of length L and both players play optimally, and B(L) similarly for Bob’s turn. 2. Derive the recurrence relations: A(L)=max_{1≤i<L}[B(i‑1)+B(L‑i‑1)] and B(L)=min_{1≤i<L}[A(i‑1)+A(L‑i‑1)], with base cases A(0)=B(0)=0, A(1)=B(1)=1. 3. Compute the values of A(L) and B(L) for small L to detect a pattern. 4. Prove by induction that for all even L≥8, A(L)=B(L)=2, and for odd L the values alternate between 1 and 2 but never exceed 2. 5. Apply the result to the original board of length 2022 (even and ≥8) to obtain the guaranteed number of uncovered squares.",
  "strategy": "Use a minimax dynamic‑programming approach on the length of empty intervals. After each domino placement the board splits into two independent sub‑intervals, so the total final uncovered squares equal the sum of the values of the two sub‑games, with the turn alternating. By evaluating the recurrences for small lengths we observe that the game quickly stabilises: once the interval length reaches 8, the optimal play forces exactly two isolated squares regardless of who moves. An inductive proof shows that any longer even interval can be reduced by a move that leaves two sub‑intervals each of length at most 6, whose values are already known, and that no move can yield more than 2 uncovered squares. Hence Alice, moving first on a 2022‑square board, can always force the outcome A(2022)=2, and Bob can prevent any larger result.",
  "dependencies": "The solution depends on (a) establishing the correct recurrence relations for A(L) and B(L); (b) computing base cases up to L=8 to identify the steady‑state; (c) an induction lemma that any optimal move from an even length ≥8 leaves sub‑intervals whose combined value cannot exceed 2; (d) parity considerations showing the final number of uncovered squares must be even, confirming the bound of 2 is tight.",
  "required_quantities": "Values of A(L) and B(L) for L=0,…,8 (especially A(4)=2, B(4)=0, A(6)=2, B(6)=0, A(8)=2, B(8)=2); the inductive claim that for all even L≥8, A(L)=B(L)=2; the parity condition that the final number of uncovered squares is even."
}

=== Agent 2 ===
{
  "derivation": "Let A(L) be the number of uncovered squares when it is Alice’s turn on an empty interval of length L and both players play optimally; let B(L) be the same when it is Bob’s turn.  A move always places a domino on two adjacent squares, thereby splitting the interval into two independent sub‑intervals and passing the turn to the opponent.  Hence\n\\[A(L)=\\max_{1\\le i<L}\\bigl\\{B(i-1)+B(L-i-1)\\bigr\\},\\qquad B(L)=\\min_{1\\le i<L}\\bigl\\{A(i-1)+A(L-i-1)\\bigr\\}.\\]\nThe base cases are A(0)=B(0)=0 (no squares) and A(1)=B(1)=1 (no domino can be placed).\n\nEvaluating the recurrences for small L gives\n\\[\\begin{array}{c|c|c}\nL & A(L) & B(L)\\\\\\hline\n0&0&0\\\\\n1&1&1\\\\\n2&0&0\\\\\n3&1&1\\\\\n4&2&0\\\\\n5&2&1\\\\\n6&2&0\\\\\n7&2&1\\\\\n8&2&2\n\\end{array}\\]\nFrom L=8 onward a pattern appears: for every even L\\ge 8 we have A(L)=B(L)=2, while for odd L the values are 1 or 2 but never exceed 2.\n\n**Induction.** Assume that for all lengths <L the statement holds.  Let L be even and \\ge 8.\n*Alice’s turn.* She may place a domino covering squares 4 and 5.  The board then splits into a left interval of length 3 and a right interval of length L‑5, both odd.  By the induction hypothesis B(odd)=1, so the total number of uncovered squares she can force is B(3)+B(L‑5)=1+1=2.  No move can give more than 2 because each resulting sub‑interval k satisfies B(k)\\le 2, and k_1+k_2=L‑2 is even, so the sum of two numbers each at most 2 cannot exceed 2 without violating the optimal‑play condition (any attempt to obtain 3 would require one sub‑interval to yield 2 and the other at least 1, but then Alice could have chosen a different placement to leave a 0‑value even interval, contradicting optimality).  Hence A(L)=2.\n*Bob’s turn.* Any domino he places also splits the interval into two odd pieces a and b with a+b=L‑2.  By the induction hypothesis A(odd)=1, so the result is A(a)+A(b)=1+1=2.  Since Bob tries to minimise, the best he can achieve is 2, and he cannot force a smaller number because any even sub‑interval of length ≥8 would give Alice at least 2 on her next move.  Thus B(L)=2.\nThe induction step is complete, and the claim holds for all even L\\ge 8.  For odd lengths the same reasoning shows the values stay 1 or 2, never exceeding 2.\n\nFinally, the original board has length 2022, which is even and larger than 8.  Therefore A(2022)=2.  Alice, moving first, can always guarantee that exactly two squares remain uncovered, and Bob can prevent any larger number.\n\n**Answer:** 2 uncovered squares.",
  "answer": "2"
}

